<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khuzestan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khuzistan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khuzestan, historically Khuzistan, is a province of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, watered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karun-river">Karun River</a>. It was the heartland of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> as its chief city, and later a rich province of the Achaemenid, Sasanian and Islamic empires, famous for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane and textiles. Today it is the centre of Iran's oil industry and has a large Arab population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khuzestan.jpg" alt="Khuzestan" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: درفش کاویانی · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shush_Castle_Darafsh_(25).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Khuzestan is an extension of the Mesopotamian Plain in the south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and covers 64,055 square kilometres. Its capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a>; the provincial capital was moved there from Shoushtar in 1924. Greek and Roman sources, from the Achaemenid period until the early centuries CE, called the land Susiana, after the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, once a capital of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and one of the Achaemenid imperial capitals. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> themselves called the satrapy Ūja or Uvja. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> and Sasanians Susiana fell out of use, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> period the name Khuzistan, from the root hūz, replaced it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Khuzestan was one of the centres of ancient civilisation and one of the most important regions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Ancient Near East</a>, based around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a>, whose language was neither Semitic nor Indo-European, ruled the first large empire here, in the 4th millennium BCE, and archaeological ruins show the entire province to have been Elamite. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> took control in 224 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> were defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hormozdgan">Battle of Hormozdgan</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> (reigned 309 or 310 to 379) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>; he led an expedition through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, defeated the combined forces of three Arab tribes and resettled them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a>.</p><p>In 639 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>'s forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-musa">Abu Musa</a> al-Ash'ari defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a> and drove out the satrap Hormuzan. Hormuzan fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shushtar">Shushtar</a>, which the Muslims besieged for 18 months until it fell in 642. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a> the western part of the province was known as Arabistan, and in the two decades before 1925 that western part, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Muhammara, functioned as an effectively autonomous emirate under Sheikh Khazʽal. Reza Shah's government dissolved it in 1925 to centralise the state, and put down the rebellion that followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> invaded Khuzestan in 1980; the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Khorramshahr</a> in 1982 reversed the invasion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Khuzestan is one of the 31 provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. It has a large Arab population and contains Iran's largest oil field. Its ancient sites include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, modern Shush, and the Sasanian works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shushtar">Shushtar</a>, and its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a> lies where the Mesopotamian Plain meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khuzestan province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Mardavij was murdered in 935, Hasan fought for years to secure Ray, Isfahan and the Jibal, while Ahmad advanced into <b>Khuzistan</b> and, in December 945, entered Baghdad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Shia Islam spread in their lands through Shaykh Junayd of the Safavid order and the Musha'sha' in <b>Khuzistan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mandaeans are an ethnoreligious group who speak Mandaic, a dialect of Eastern Aramaic, and live in southern Iraq and the Iranian province of <b>Khuzestan</b> and in diaspora communities, with 60,000 to 70,000 worldwide.</blockquote><a href="/religion/gnosticism">Gnosticism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The religion has been practised mainly around the lower Karun, Euphrates and Tigris and the rivers of the Shatt al-Arab, in southern Iraq and <b>Khuzestan</b> in Iran.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mandaeism">Mandaeism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Some 600 years after its creation the stele was taken as plunder to Susa, the ancient Elamite city in what is now <b>Khuzestan</b> Province, Iran.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Ya'qub abandoned his attempt to take Iraq and withdrew to <b>Khuzistan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-8">8 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
